    (' \\bigl(1 + R^{(\\mathrm{photon})}(\\tau)\\bigr)\\,d\\tau\n \\;=\\; \\bsym^2\\cdot(\\lP^{(\\mathrm{photon})})^2.\n\\end{equation}\n\n\\begin{remark}[Regularization required]\n\\label{rem:regularization-B}\nThe integrand $1 + R(\\tau)$ has a pole at $\\tau=0$: since\n$R(\\tau) = -e^{-\\tau/2}/(e^\\tau-1) \\sim -1/\\tau + 1/2 - \\tau/12 +\n\\cdots$ as $\\tau\\to 0^+$, the combination $1 + R(\\tau) \\sim\n3/2 - 1/\\tau + \\cdots$ is still singular.\nEquation~\\eqref{eq:balance-reduced} must therefore be read as a\n\\emph{renormalized} integral:\n\\[\n \\mathrm{p.v.}\\int_0^T (1 + R(\\tau))\\,d\\tau\n \\;:=\\;\n \\lim_{\\varepsilon\\to 0}\n \\Bigl[\n \\int_\\varepsilon^T (1+R(\\tau))\\,d\\tau + \\log\\varepsilon\n \\Bigr],\n\\]\nwhere the $\\log\\varepsilon$ counterterm removes the leading pole.\nNumerically with $T = \\lP^{(\\mathrm{photon})} \\approx 0.393$\n(matter-Planck units):\n\\[\n \\mathrm{p.v.}\\int_0^T (1+R)\\,d\\tau\n \\;=\\; \\frac{3T}{2} - \\log T - \\frac{T^2}{24} - \\gamma_E\n \\;\\approx\\; 0.940,\n\\]\nwhere $\\gamma_E \\approx 0.5772$ is the Euler--Mascheroni constant.\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\begin{proposition}[Step~(B) open: required Theta contribution]\n\\label{prop:theta-required-B}\n\\emph{(Open sub-step; not yet proven.)}\nFor the balance condition~\\eqref{eq:tr-balance} to hold, the full\nTheta sum (integrated over all zeros, not just those below\n$T_{\\mathrm{photon}}$) must contribute:\n\\[\n \\int_0^{\\lP^{(\\mathrm{photon})}}\n \\Theta^{(\\mathrm{photon})}(\\tau)\\,d\\tau\n \\;=\\;\n \\mathrm{p.v.}\\int_0^T (1+R)\\,d\\tau - \\bsym^2\\cdot T^2\n \\;\\approx\\; 0.940 - 0.302\\cdot 0.154\n \\;\\approx\\; 0.893.\n\\]\nThis integral runs over all photon-frame zeros $\\gamma_\\rho^{(\\mathrm{photon})}$\n\\emph{including those above} $T_{\\mathrm{photon}}$, which oscillate\nrapidly in the integration range $[0, T]$ and must be summed\ncarefully.\nEstablishing this cancellation constitutes the remaining hard\nmathematical content of OP~5.\n\\end{proposition}\n\n\\begin{remark}[Structural summary of OP~5]\n\\label{rem:op5-structural-summary}\nThe analysis of \\QSCver{v2.29.0} reveals the following structure of OP~5:\n\\begin{itemize}\n \\item Step~(A) is closed: $\\Theta^{(\\mathrm{photon})}=0$ below\n the photon-frame cutoff (Proposition~\\ref{prop:theta-remainder-trivial}).\n \\item Step~(B) reduces to showing that the oscillatory Theta sum,\n integrated over the photon-frame UV range $[0,\\lP^{(\\mathrm{photon})}]$\n but summing over all zeros (including those above the cutoff),\n evaluates to $\\approx 0.893$ in renormalized Planck units.\n \\item The $10^{-122}$ apparent fine-tuning is a unit-system\n artifact (Remark~\\ref{rem:open}): in natural Planck units the\n required cancellation is from $\\sim 0.940$ (R-sector baseline)\n to $0.302$ (frame residual), a factor of $\\sim 3.1$, not $10^{122}$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\subsubsection*{Step (B) reduced: the exact Theta-sum conjecture}',
     ' \\bigl(1 + R^{(\\mathrm{photon})}(\\tau)\\bigr)\\,d\\tau\n \\;=\\; \\bsym^2\\cdot(\\lP^{(\\mathrm{photon})})^2.\n\\end{equation}\n\n\\begin{remark}[Regularization required; corrected in 4.10]\n\\label{rem:regularization-B}\nFor dimensionless $\\tau>0$,\n\\[\n R(\\tau)=-\\frac{e^{-\\tau/2}}{e^\\tau-1}\n =-\\frac1\\tau+1-\\frac{11\\tau}{24}+O(\\tau^2).\n\\]\nThe one-sided endpoint singularity requires a specified finite-part\nprescription, not an ordinary convergent integral. For a positive\ndimensionless reference scale $\\mu$, define\n\\[\n \\operatorname{FP}_{\\mu}\\int_0^T(1+R(\\tau))\\,d\\tau\n =\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n \\left[\\int_\\varepsilon^T(1+R(\\tau))\\,d\\tau\n                   -\\log(\\varepsilon/\\mu)\\right].\n\\]\nA direct antiderivative gives\n\\[\n T-2e^{-T/2}+\\log\\!\\frac{1+e^{-T/2}}{1-e^{-T/2}}\n       +2-\\log4+\\log\\mu.\n\\]\nFor $T=0.393$ and $\\mu=1$ this is approximately $1.686953464$.\nChanging $\\mu$ changes the finite part. Selecting this scale or\ninterpreting the result as physical vacuum energy needs a separate\naction, units and matching prescription.\n\\end{remark}\n\\qsstatus{4.10 correction: the earlier expansion\n$-1/\\tau+1/2-\\tau/12$, the added $+\\log\\varepsilon$ counterterm,\nand the value $0.940$ were incorrect. The added counterterm reinforced\nthe endpoint divergence. The withdrawn Theta-balance route is not\nreinstated by this correction.}\n\n\\begin{proposition}[Step~(B): historical target withdrawn]\n\\label{prop:theta-required-B}\nThe former numerical target $0.893=0.940-0.302\\cdot0.154$ depended\non the incorrect finite-part evaluation in\nRemark~\\ref{rem:regularization-B}. It is not an established requirement\non a physical Theta contribution. The subsequent balance conjecture is\nalready withdrawn in Proposition~\\ref{conj:theta-sum-B}.\nA corrected finite-part convention alone neither restores that balance\nnor closes OP5.\n\\end{proposition}\n\\qsstatus{4.10 correction: the preceding target is retained here only\nas a historical dependency. No new cancellation target is inferred.}\n\n\\begin{remark}[Current status of the historical OP5 route]\n\\label{rem:op5-structural-summary}\nThe former numerical summary using $0.940$ and $0.893$ is superseded\nby the finite-part correction above and the already withdrawn\nTheta-sum conjecture. A dimensionless discrepancy is not removed by a\nchange of units. The newer state, clock and field comparisons in\nSection~\\ref{sec:v410-state-mediator} identify conditional structures\nand remaining compatibility conditions; they do not prove cancellation\nof a cosmological term. OP5 remains open.\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\subsubsection*{Step (B) reduced: the exact Theta-sum conjecture}'),
